      "body": "## Motivation\n\nSince #47162 (merged 2026-10-29), MI300 jobs on ROCm 10 take about 3 minutes to start their container, up from about 6 seconds. It started at 19:00 UTC that day, when that day's MI300 image was published.\n\nWhy:\n\n- #47162 changed `amd_ci_start_container.sh` to always prefer the newest image on Docker Hub.\n- Each ARC runner is used for one job and then deleted. Images it pulls are not kept for the next job.\n- Runners start with a copy of a per-node warm store that holds one image (`rocm10-mi30x`). The store is refreshed about 5 hours 20 minutes after a new image is published (for example, the 2026-10-29 image was published at 19:06 UTC, and the nodes refreshed at 00:26-00:29 UTC).\n- So from each publish until the next refresh, every MI300 ROCm 10 job downloads the 63 GB image again through the Dragonfly mirror, taking about 3 minutes. That is about 5 hours a day, roughly 40 extra runner hours a day.\n\n## Modifications\n\nFor `pull_request` runs only, use an image that is already on the runner if it is at most 2 days old (`PR_LOCAL_IMAGE_MAX_AGE_DAYS`, default 2). Otherwise, fall through to the current newest-first logic.\n\nScheduled, nightly and manual runs are unchanged and still take the newest image.\n\nPR jobs install sglang from the PR checkout and build `sgl-kernel` from it, so an image up to 2 days old is fine for them. The image still provides AITER, Triton, torch, ROCm and the Dockerfile settings.\n\nChecked locally with a stub `docker`:\n\n| Run | On the runner | On Docker Hub | Picked |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| PR | yesterday | today | yesterday (no pull) |\n| PR | 3 days old | today | today (pull) |\n| schedule | yesterday | today | today (pull) |\n| schedule | yesterday | yesterday only | yesterday (no pull) |\n\n## Accuracy Tests\n\nN/A (CI script only).\n\n## Speed Tests and Profiling\n\nN/A.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
